```
(88 bytes; `...argument'\x0a\x20\x20what():\x20\x20stoi\x0a`)

### Path C — `std::stoi` out of `int` range

```
terminate called after throwing an instance of 'std::out_of_range'\n  what():  stoi\n
```
(82 bytes)

### Spacing rules (do not get these wrong)

- Line 1: `terminate called after throwing an instance of '<TYPE>'` then `\n`. Single quotes around the type. No period.
- Line 2: **two** leading spaces, then `what():`, then **two** spaces, then the message, then `\n`.
- No trailing space anywhere; exactly one `\n` terminating each of the two lines; no third line.

---

## 4. Reproducing the abort paths in Rust

```rust
use std::io::Write;

const MSG_LOGIC: &str = "terminate called after throwing an instance of 'std::logic_error'\n  what():  basic_string::_S_construct null not valid\n";
const MSG_INVALID: &str = "terminate called after throwing an instance of 'std::invalid_argument'\n  what():  stoi\n";
const MSG_RANGE: &str = "terminate called after throwing an instance of 'std::out_of_range'\n  what():  stoi\n";

fn die(msg: &str) -> ! {
    let mut e = std::io::stderr();
    let _ = e.write_all(msg.as_bytes());
    let _ = e.flush();
    std::process::abort();
}
```

Empirically confirmed:
- A tiny Rust program doing `eprint!(<the two lines>); std::process::abort();` produces stderr **byte-identical** to the C++ binary (verified with `xxd`) and the shell reports `rc=134` with `Aborted (core dumped)`.
- `std::process::abort()` prints nothing of its own.
- Do **not** use `panic!` (prints a Rust panic message, exits 101) and do **not** use `std::process::exit(134)` (that is a normal exit with status 134, not a signal death — `$?` looks the same in bash but `WIFSIGNALED` differs, and any harness using `subprocess` / `wait4` will see `returncode == 134` vs `-6`; abort() gives `-6`, matching C++).
- Use `write_all` + `flush` (or `eprint!`, which is unbuffered) so the bytes land before the signal.

---

## 5. stdout formatting (success path)

`std::cout << size << " " << bucket_count << " " << overflow_size << std::endl;`

Exact form: `"<size> <bucket_count> <overflow_size>\n"` — single ASCII space separators, **no trailing space**, exactly one trailing `\n`, nothing else. `std::endl` flushes; there is no extra flush artifact.

Hex-verified:
```
no args      : 3138 2033 3220 300a                 -> "18 32 0\n"      (8 bytes)
--a 0        : 3020 3020 300a                      -> "0 0 0\n"        (6 bytes)
--a 1000000  : 3130 3030 3030 3020 3230 3937 3135 3220 300a -> "1000000 2097152 0\n"
```

Rust: `println!("{} {} {}", size, bucket_count, 0u64);` — `LineWriter` flushes on the `\n`, and std flushes stdout at exit. All three values print as unsigned decimal (`size_type` = `unsigned long`); `overflow_size` is always literally `0` (see §6).

---

## 6. Numeric model — CORRECTED (this is the important one)

Reverse-engineered by bisecting three power-of-two boundaries on the real binary:

```
size         = if count > 0 { count as u64 } else { 0 }
overflow     = 0   (always)
bucket_count = round_up_pow2( ceil_f64( count / D ) )   with D = f64::from(0.9f32)
```

where `D = 0.9f32 as f64 = 0.89999997615814208984375 = 7549747 / 2^23` (exact), and
`round_up_pow2(0) == 0`, `round_up_pow2(x) == smallest power of two >= x` (identity on exact powers of two).

The stored `max_load_factor` is the **`float` literal `0.9f`**, but the division in `reserve()` is performed in **`double`** with the *exact integer* numerator — i.e. `static_cast<size_type>(std::ceil(static_cast<double>(count) / max_load_factor()))`.

### Proof / discriminating data (bisected on the binary)

Every boundary sits on an exact multiple of 7549747 (because `2^k * 7549747/2^23` is an integer for `k >= 23`):

| N | reference | f32 model (lead's) | f64 with `0.9f32 as f64` | f64 with `0.9` (double) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 30198988 (=4·7549747) | 33554432 | 33554432 | 33554432 | 33554432 |
| 30198989 | 67108864 | **33554432 ✗** | 67108864 | 67108864 |
| 60397976 (=8·7549747) | 67108864 | 67108864 | 67108864 | 67108864 |
| 60397977 | 134217728 | **67108864 ✗** | 134217728 | **67108864 ✗** |
| 120795952 (=16·7549747) | 134217728 | 134217728 | 134217728 | 134217728 |
| 120795953 | 268435456 | **134217728 ✗** | 268435456 | **134217728 ✗** |
| 120795954/5 | 268435456 | **134217728 ✗** | 268435456 | **134217728 ✗** |
| 120795956 | 268435456 | **134217728 ✗** | 268435456 | 268435456 |

Only the middle column reproduces all reference outputs. The f32 model coincides with reality for every `N <= 30198988`, which is why the lead's small-N table looked consistent.

### Small-N table re-verified against the binary (all match the corrected model)

`0->0 0 0`, `1->1 2 0`, `2->2 4 0`, `3->3 4 0`, `4->4 8 0`, `5..7->8`, `8..14->16`, `15..28->32`, `29..57->64`, `58..115->128`, `116..230->256`, `231..460->512`, `461..1023->1024`, `1000->2048`, `1024->2048`, `1843->2048`, `1844->4096`, `1000000->2097152`, `2147483647->4294967296`.

### Negative `count`

`reserve(size_type(negative))` sign-extends; `double(huge)/D ~= 2.05e19 >= 2^64`; the C++ `double -> size_t` conversion is UB and on x86-64 (gcc's `sub 2^63 / cvttsd2si / add 2^63` sequence) yields **0**. Result: `bucket_count = 0`, loop body never executes, output `0 0 0`, rc 0. Confirmed for `-1, -2, -3, -100, -2000000, -2147483646, -2147483647, -2147483648, -0, -000000000000005, -0x5`.

Because the *only* reachable regimes are `count >= 0` (tiny quotient) and `count < 0` (quotient `>= 2^64` -> 0), the safe, provably-equivalent Rust form is:

```rust
let (size, bucket_count): (u64, u64) = if count <= 0 {
    (0, 0)
} else {
    let target = ((count as f64) / (0.9f32 as f64)).ceil() as u64;
    (count as u64, if target == 0 { 0 } else { target.next_power_of_two() })
};
```

Rust pitfalls here:
- `0u64.next_power_of_two() == 1`, **not 0** — the `target == 0` guard is mandatory (`--a 0` must print `0 0 0`).
- Rust's `f64 as u64` **saturates** to `u64::MAX`; it does NOT reproduce the C++ UB-yields-0. Hence the explicit `count <= 0` branch rather than trying to cast `2.05e19`.
- Do **not** replace the f64 division with exact rational arithmetic (`ceil(count * 8388608 / 7549747)` in `u128`). The reference rounds the quotient to nearest `f64` *before* `ceil`, and at magnitudes near `2.4e9` the f64 rounding error (~2.6e-7) can exceed the minimum distance to an integer (`1/7549747 ~= 1.32e-7`). Mirror the float arithmetic exactly.

### `overflow_size()` is always 0

`std::hash<int>` in libstdc++ is the identity, the growth policy is power-of-two masking, and `bucket_count >= ceil(count/0.9) >= count`, so keys `0..count-1` map to `count` distinct home buckets, each of which is free when its key arrives. No neighborhood ever overflows. Verified 0 across all 362 test cases including `count = 1000000` and `count = 120795956`.

### Practical warning about huge `count`

`--a 2147483647` makes the C++ binary allocate `2^32` buckets (~50 GB): on one run it completed in ~45 s printing `2147483647 4294967296 0`; on a repeat it was **OOM-killed (`rc=137`, no output)**. `--a 1000000` takes 24 ms, `--a 10000000` 197 ms. Recommendation: the Rust port must compute the three numbers **arithmetically** and not build a real hash map — otherwise it inherits the same nondeterministic OOM, and it cannot match the "correct" output for large N. (Analytic computation also makes `--a 2147483647` instant and deterministic, which is the intended output.)

---

## 7. Validated end-to-end prototype

`/tmp/rtest/p.rs` — compiled with `rustc -O --edition 2021 p.rs -o p`, no external crates:

```rust
use std::io::Write;

const LOGIC: &str = "terminate called after throwing an instance of 'std::logic_error'\n  what():  basic_string::_S_construct null not valid\n";
const INVAL: &str = "terminate called after throwing an instance of 'std::invalid_argument'\n  what():  stoi\n";
const RANGE: &str = "terminate called after throwing an instance of 'std::out_of_range'\n  what():  stoi\n";

fn die(m: &str) -> ! {
    let mut e = std::io::stderr();
    let _ = e.write_all(m.as_bytes());
    let _ = e.flush();
    std::process::abort();
}

fn stoi(b: &[u8]) -> i32 {
    let mut i = 0usize;
    while i < b.len() && matches!(b[i], b' ' | b'\t' | b'\n' | 0x0b | 0x0c | b'\r') { i += 1; }
    let mut neg = false;
    if i < b.len() && (b[i] == b'+' || b[i] == b'-') { neg = b[i] == b'-'; i += 1; }
    let st = i;
    let mut acc: u64 = 0;
    while i < b.len() && b[i].is_ascii_digit() {
        if acc < (1u64 << 40) { acc = acc * 10 + (b[i] - b'0') as u64; }
        i += 1;
    }
    if i == st { die(INVAL); }
    let v: i128 = if neg { -(acc as i128) } else { acc as i128 };
    if v < i32::MIN as i128 || v > i32::MAX as i128 { die(RANGE); }
    v as i32
}

fn main() {
    use std::os::unix::ffi::OsStrExt;
    let a: Vec<std::ffi::OsString> = std::env::args_os().collect();
    let mut count: i32 = 18;
    if a.len() > 1 && a[1].as_os_str() == "--a" {
        if a.len() < 3 { die(LOGIC); }
        count = stoi(a[2].as_bytes());
    }
    let (size, bc) = if count <= 0 {
        (0u64, 0u64)
    } else {
        let t = ((count as f64) / (0.9f32 as f64)).ceil() as u64;
        (count as u64, if t == 0 { 0 } else { t.next_power_of_two() })
    };
    println!("{} {} {}", size, bc, 0u64);
}
